WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.348
Aerial observation is structured, purpose-driven visual collection from above.

00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:16.300
It can use drones, aircraft, towers, elevated cameras, or overhead imagery, but every method requires context, authority, and careful interpretation.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:24.704
Begin with a question, bounded area, required detail, time window, environmental constraints, and explicit exclusions.

00:00:24.704 --> 00:00:33.109
Plan rules, airspace, property access, weather, lighting, obstacles, route, battery margins, recovery, and emergency options.

00:00:33.109 --> 00:00:42.532
Collect stable, well-framed imagery with an angle, field of view, overlap, and format suited to the objective.

00:00:42.532 --> 00:00:52.465
Describe visible features before interpreting them, and never infer identity, intent, ownership, cause, or severity without supporting evidence.

00:00:52.465 --> 00:01:00.360
Time-based comparison depends on repeatable collection and preserved date, location, sensor, and condition context.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:08.764
File naming, metadata, logs, source notes, controlled originals, access restrictions, and retention make findings reviewable.

00:01:08.764 --> 00:01:16.659
The goal is useful situational awareness and evidence, not uncontrolled surveillance or instant certainty.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:23.536
Strong practice combines safety, image quality, documentation, privacy, proportionality, and responsible reporting.
